{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '298,427p' collab/jolt/1794773141000-architect-j7-design.findings.md && rg -n '8.4|J7|ENOBUFS|retry|nonblocking' docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md && cat src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc && git diff --no-index /dev/null src/cljc/dao/stream/udp/common.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '298,427p' collab/jolt/1794773141000-architect-j7-design.findings.md && rg -n '8.4|J7|ENOBUFS|retry|nonblocking' docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md && cat src/cljc/dao/stream/udp.cljc && git diff --no-index /dev/null src/cljc/dao/stream/udp/common.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"## 5. Implementation slices with falsifiable acceptance\n\nThese are J7 subdivisions, not replacement names for the governing J0/J6\nslices. Each lands with its own tests/evidence; platform completion is tracked\nseparately. Keep the shell's blanket jolt refusal through J7c.\n\n| Slice / order | Change boundary | Required acceptance before moving on |\n|---|---|---|\n| J7a — preserve the portable surface and qualify prerequisites | Factor UDP value code/facades for §2; lower qualified entropy adapter, retaining existing signing entry points. Refresh the J0b baseline on the pinned runtime. | Existing `udp-test`, `udp-raw-test`, `datagram-test` behavior and exported API/arities remain unchanged on JVM/Node/Dart and jolt. The host-free raw/value suites currently excluded as “UDP sockets” actually run. Random draw failure is explicit, no secret or syscall fallback; signing vectors remain unchanged. Clean tracked-root loading on all hosts; no jolt → Transit or dao.space → yin dependency. |\n| J7b — native datagram implementation | Raw helpers and binder in `src/jolt/dao/stream/udp.jolt`; bounded owner-driven `:step!`, lifecycle and cleanup. | All §6 J0b legs against production helpers, plus jolt writer/traffic-ring contract tests: IPv4/IPv6 actual-port round trips, source fidelity, invalid-value with zero syscall count, size/drop cases, failure pair, family mismatch, idempotent close, slow-reader gap and two independent seams. Inject EAGAIN/ENOBUFS/short send and count exactly one syscall and one failure event per attempt; subsequent real successful syscall works. No packet appears later from a failed attempt. Bounded flood turn and empty turn return without waiting. |\n| J7c — shared DHT host composition | Explicit jolt default binder/entropy selection; optional seam servicing before `dao.space.dht` protocol step. Plain `join`/store/announce/load/query use the existing shared algorithms. | On actual native loopback sockets, small and chunked content exchange, cookies/source matching, lost/reordered/inconsistent chunks, configured inbound bound, fetch-only mode, bind/send failure propagation, and a caller-driven plain `join` load/query pass. Solo opens no fd/secret/pending network work. Local put/readback needs no transport step; failed sends do not count a peer, including failure halfway through a chunk run. Persistent reopen and owner cleanup pass. Retain the existing three-socket fixture; do not substitute a mesh-only result for real UDP. |\n| J7d — shell and signed-head activation | Apply §4's board profiles, remove `checked-spec`'s jolt throw, use the existing factory/session/linker composition and J6 WS host. | A real jolt `dht:<dir>` shell publishes, persists HEAD, queries after restart and exits with all resources released. A manifest-pinned reader hydrates before executing pretyped input; signed-name require works on every supported VM. A loopback signed-board follower observes a move, persists it before resolver installation, and restores its rollback floor on restart. Tampering, bad key, HEAD write failure, corrupt heads file, UDP bind failure, TCP-board bind failure, hydration failure and forced shutdown retain their specified outcomes. Re-enable the J7-owned shell per-test exclusions. |\n| J7e — parity evidence and closure | Add jolt child roles to the real-process DHT/signed-head harness, and reconcile applicability entries and source-load checks. No new transport implementation. | Complete §7 on macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64: jolt in each relevant role against JVM, Node and Dart, real UDP and native WS boards, executed slow bodies and full summaries. Restore shared deterministic suites, retain eight distinct manifest publications and twenty-round/400-tick idle proof, and demonstrate idle/headless progress without stdin. No open J7 prerequisite is hidden by a skip; unaffected three-host lanes and engine Gate A/B stay green. Record each platform's commands, revisions, counts, outcomes and cleanup. |\n\nJ7d may be tested as an activation candidate before committing it; do not ship\na broadly accepted spec whose follow/publish options are silently ignored.\nIf a platform or required role remains unrun, the implementation can be\nreported as partial, but §10.1's J7 gate remains open.\n\n## 6. J0b regressions retained\n\n`bb probe:jolt-udp` already exists (`bb.edn:70`), driving tracked\n`src/dev/jolt_udp_probe.{clj,sh}`. Preserve its assertions and diagnostic\nrecords. The old shell wrapper can fall back to an ignored spike checkout;\nJ7 qualification must explicitly use the bootstrapped tracked toolchain and\nrecord its SHA/clean state rather than relying on that fallback.\n\n| §8.3 leg | Retained proof / evidence required for J7 |\n|---|---|\n| 1 — boundaries | Nonblocking loopback bind and ephemeral-port readback; exact payload AND source for empty, embedded-NUL, consecutive distinct, and N-sized packets; one receive consumes at most one packet. Inject short send: error, no suffix send and no accepted packet. Repeat through production helpers and the public writer. |\n| 2 — bounds | N=1200 and configured N=8192, asserting the latter is below the measured send maximum; N, N+1 and packets larger than the receive buffer. No accepted prefix, consumed oversize packet leaves no remainder. Separately induce above-platform-limit EMSGSIZE and observe no delivery. Preserve platform header/sysctl/MTU and socket-buffer readback evidence. Add IPv6 coverage in J7 without pretending it existed in J0b. |\n| 3 — pressure | Raw would-block classification retains errno. Inject target EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK and ENOBUFS with zero accepted packets; also exercise actual successful syscalls before/after. Natural pressure trials remain RECORD observations, including packet loss; no requirement to manufacture natural loopback EAGAIN. At the public writer assert transport-error plus send-failed, no `full`, no wait and no replay on later turns. |\n| 4 — readiness / cleanup | Keep the distinct raw probe's read waiter: it parks through the poller, another fiber progresses, a packet wakes it, cancel releases it, close/forget removes descriptor state, post-close receive reports EBADF. The harness may wait/retry to make observations; those loops must not enter production `append!` or receive turns. Test production failed-open cleanup, repeated close, no retained fd/native buffers and descriptor reuse separately. |\n| 5 — reproducibility | Separate macOS arm64/Linux x86_64 records: repository and runtime SHA, Chez/compiler/native provenance, platform and header constants, exact command/exit, packet sizes/counts, raw error traces, injected vs natural labels, poller traces and final empty ownership state. Missing machine/output is incomplete, not passed. |\n\nHistorical evidence exists, not a fresh acceptance result:\n\n- [macOS round 2](1792429967000-engineer-jolt-j0b-udp-probe-r2.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md)\n  records 36 passing assertions and three observations on jolt `96d7b866`;\n  IPv4 maximum observed there was 9216 bytes.\n- [Linux review/sign-off](1792487422000-reviewer-jolt-j0b-linux.qwen3.8-max.findings.md)\n  records shiva runs with 37 passing assertions and three observations,\n  maximum 65507 and EMSGSIZE 90. The extra assertion is Linux SO_SNDBUF\n  readback. Both platforms used injection for send pressure/short-send paths.\n- [tracked probe transfer](1792489765000-engineer-jolt-j0b-commit-prep.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md)\n  records the move into `src/dev`. The governing table's old J0b\n  “probe-only; not merged” label is not evidence the tracked files are absent,\n  and the old runs are not datagram/DHT conformance.\n\nCurrent `jolt-toolchain.edn` pins `b389f1a7fb553f63a3494d71f8fe3251790bf4d3`,\nnot the probe's historical SHA (nor the older SHA in `src/jolt/README.md`).\nRerun the retained legs at the actual qualified pin on both platforms.\nThe J6 macOS Autobahn waiver does not waive J7 UDP or signed-head evidence.\n\n## 7. J7 gate mapped to §10.1\n\n| Governing J7 requirement | Concrete gate / evidence owner |\n|---|---|\n| “§8.4” datagram/address/size/lifecycle integration | J7b: existing `test/dao/stream/datagram_test.cljc`, a new native jolt seam suite/probe, preserved UDP value/raw suites. Check actual ring values, maximum policy, IPv4/IPv6 and observed-source addressing. |\n| “J0b regressions retained” | J7a/J7b: every row of §6; retain the separate poller/fiber leg and run native packet/error legs through the production implementation too. Mere copying of the probe or requiring its namespace does not pass. |\n| “would-block/ENOBUFS is transport-error plus send-failed” | J7b/J7c: deterministic native injection, exact writer outcome and one traffic event, zero packets accepted and zero later resend; a DHT chunk run cannot count that destination as sent. |\n| “no full, no hidden retries” | J7b: syscall counters under pressure/short/error; no outbound buffer/queue or send-side wait; no POLLOUT-driven replay. J7c: existing explicit DHT repair remains above the seam and retains its original retry policy. |\n| DHT/linker composition, real processes and signed heads | J7c/J7d/J7e: `dao.space.dht-test`, `yin.vm.linker.dht-test`, `dht-end-to-end-test`, `head-test`, `head-follow-test`, `head-board-test`, `yin.repl.dht-test`, `dht-head-test` and jolt roles in `dht_process_test.clj`. Adapt codec-neutral board fixtures to CBOR on jolt; do not load Transit to make them pass. |\n| Bind/send failures and shutdown | J7b/J7d: UDP failure pair and socket ownership; shell refusal for failed UDP bind; failed TCP board yields no token; publication/storage failure ordering, follow persistence failure, graceful drain and forced final cleanup. Zero live UDP/TCP descriptors, dials, owned native buffers and directory lock after close. |\n| Host parity; deferral remains incomplete | J7e: independent macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 records, jolt publisher/reader/follower against each JVM/Node/Dart peer, both directions where roles differ, all using updated CBOR-capable board composition. A JVM test driver is allowed; the deployed jolt child has no JVM/Node helper. Required peer build unavailable is a failed/incomplete gate. |\n\nThe process harness is currently JVM-controlled and starts JVM/Node roles\n(`test/yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj`); add configurable jolt launch roles\nusing the tracked `:jolt:jolt-yin-repl` alias rather than porting ProcessBuilder\nor the anchor thread to jolt. Include jolt-to-jolt durable publication and\nrestart, then interop in both storage/load roles. Test `::1` raw transport\nseparately from any IPv4-specific process transcript regex; changing a regex\ndoes not establish IPv6 transport acceptance.\n\nReconcile `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj` and\n`test/resources/yin/repl/jolt-applicability.edn`: run portable DHT, UDP and\nhead suites previously excluded for unavailable transport, and remove J7's\nper-deftest refusals once their bodies pass. Some exclusions falsely label\nhost-free suites as “UDP sockets”; they are not substitutes for real-socket\nevidence, but they should not remain skipped. Genuine JVM/Node/Dart host\nfixtures may stay in their own lanes with precise capability reasons and\nthe native jolt equivalent named. Do not blanket-delete other tracks' entries\nor declare their capabilities supported by a J7 label.\n\nPreserve the DHT-composition spec's stronger retained proofs: eight **distinct\nmanifest addresses**, the twenty-round/400-tick idle fixture, corruption and\nrecovery, and follow persistence. Neither timing relaxations nor acknowledgement\ntimestamp counts replace those assertions. Keep engine Gate A's zero backend\nreferences and Gate B's injected non-DHT storage suites: no backend knowledge\nis added to `yin.repl`, and no network waiting is added to local publication.\nShared-source and facade factoring changes require the affected JVM/Node/Dart\nlanes in addition to jolt. J8 still owns full repository lane qualification;\nJ7 cannot defer its own datagram/DHT acceptance to J8.\n\n## 8. Explicitly out of scope\n\n- Multi-peer testnet topology design, deployment/orchestration, large-network\n  routing experiments and availability campaigns. Required bounded loopback\n  fixtures, including the existing three-socket test and ack-peers process\n  scenarios, remain acceptance tests; this exclusion does not remove them.\n- NAT traversal, hole punching, STUN/TURN, meeting services, automatic public\n  address discovery, DNS resolution, and scoped IPv6 zone ids. A bind address\n  does not become an advertised reachable address.\n- A new DHT protocol, routing algorithm, chunk wire, cookie scheme, reliability\n  layer or UDP outbound queue; changing replication/repair thresholds without\n  the existing measurement proof; new index/manifest/heads schemas.\n- WS TLS, Transit on jolt, browser transport, Windows qualification, sidecars,\n  a replacement file store or handwritten signing/hash primitives.\n- Broad engine refactoring, a new host entropy profile, immediate UDP wakeup\n  optimization, or a new universal transport runner. The explicit seam turn\n  and small host-code factoring above are sufficient for this slice.\n\n## 9. Design verification and implementation handoff\n\nRead the requested governing sections and traced their present factory,\ndatagram writer, native TCP/pump, crypto/entropy, durable filesystem, board\ncodec, process harness and applicability boundaries at the recorded base.\nThe principal findings are the absent production UDP seam/step, the namespace\ncollision at the requested landing path, the unqualified entropy fallback,\nand the Transit-only shell board selection. All have concrete dispositions\nand acceptance above.\n\nThis artifact changes no source or test behavior. No transport, remote host,\nor runtime suite was executed for this architecture round; historical results\nare attributed in §6 and all J7 acceptance remains prospective. Artifact\nvalidation checks relative Markdown links, existing versus explicitly proposed\nsource paths, balanced code fences and whitespace; cited source boundaries\nwere checked against the base. The implementation findings must report actual commands, counts, failures,\nplatform gaps and the final applicability changes, rather than treating this\ndesign as a network-parity sign-off.\n219:   J6/J7. Not parity with the network-capable lanes.\n234:| `yin.repl/create-state` (`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:814`) and `eval-input` (`:2095`) | Same options, results, output events, pending behaviour and errors. Direct core    |\n375:| `src/cljc/yin/vm/integer/host.cljc:27-28,41-108,115-298`              | Import, carrier predicates, promotion/demotion, truncation, arithmetic, compare, three big bit |\n431:|                                | retry can answer success for data that never reached storage.                                                           |             |\n458:| Sockets                        | Native POSIX sockets through `jolt.socket.native` FFI and `jolt.io-poller` directly (UDP: S on macOS, J0b; TCP: R).    | J0b, J6, J7 |\n461:| UDP                            | Narrow nonblocking SOCK_DGRAM adapter under the landed datagram contract (§8).                                          | J0b, J7     |\n572:  CLJS/CLJD (`src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:38-43`; r7 cited `:44-50`, which has\n716:native-library provenance; J6/J7 peers once they exist. It records candidate,\n765:`collab/1792824446211-engineer-canonical-adjudication-fixes.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md`\n1201:agrees (r7 cite). So EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK, and ENOBUFS on macOS, produce\n1203:`append!` or a retry. Attribution: the owner's original instruction was only\n1215:nonblocking TCP layer over `jolt.io-poller` that reads a recv error as EOF;\n1221:P: add and verify SOCK_DGRAM, EMSGSIZE and the macOS ENOBUFS constants from\n1224:nonblocking and cleanup helpers; never a helper that hardcodes SOCK_STREAM.\n1231:1. Bind nonblocking SOCK_DGRAM loopback sockets through FFI and read back the\n1246:3. Send EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK (and macOS ENOBUFS) appear as a distinct raw result,\n1262:### 8.4 J7 integration\n1264:J7 integrates the probed transport through the datagram host seam:\n1315:|                                                                  | retryable `false`/`full` (V: `jvm.clj:231-239`). The JVM client cannot send 1009 (V:                |\n1344:- a nonblocking partial-write queue with exact `:queued-bytes`;\n1361:now, from an exact `:queued-bytes`, which only the adapter's own nonblocking\n1530:| probe-only; not merged                      | transport on      | landed transport-error and send-failed on EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK and macOS ENOBUFS, nonblocking         |\n1580:| (890dc68f, 9c4ce1df,                        | and key files,    | work. J4d: the file: store opens on jolt (only dht: refuses, at composition, J7); saved flags       |\n1606:| J7: UDP and DHT                             | Integrate the J0b | §8.4; J0b regressions retained; would-block/ENOBUFS is transport-error plus send-failed; no `full`, |\n1613:| not started                                 | and subsystem     | and J1-J7 gate on both platforms; existing three lanes green; same shared kit, no overlays/stubs,   |\n1639:transfer the J7 UDP no-`full` policy to WebSocket or change WS backpressure.\n1648:`05a596c5`; gate `j6ggate-fixrun2` on jupiter against the watchdog wire-format fix `8c4bf09c`): 600-sample prospective cohort, 0 violations,\n1665:J1 depends on J0a; J5 on J0c plus the correctness and CLI slices; J7 on J0b. A\n1690:durable stores until J4/J7, and runs one plain-thread owner through `run-owner!`.\n1795:| Q1         | \"aceept local-REPL milestone first\"   | The local REPL milestone (through J5) comes first; J6/J7 are later parity work.                                  |\n1813:| Q7         | \"keep landed contract\"                | No UDP `full`. Would-block (EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK, macOS ENOBUFS) is transport-error plus the send-failed event; no |\n1814:|            |                                       | queue, park or retry in `append!`.                                                                               |\n1911:  native+poller substrate on macOS for UDP: nonblocking fds, parking, wakeup,\n2036:| R3      | UDP attribution corrected; landed transport-error plus lifecycle event; no             | §8.1, §8.3, J0b, J7, Q7                                            |\n2112:| `src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc:38-43`                 | Default ref type `:soft` on JVM, `:strong` on CLJS/CLJD. Confirmed at these lines; r7's `:44-50` has drifted.   |\n2122:| `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:275-279,814,2095`            | JVM banner arm; `create-state`; `eval-input`. Confirmed.                                                        |\n2138:| jolt `stdlib/jolt/socket.clj:12-23`                  | Recv error reads as EOF; nonblocking over `jolt.io-poller`. Confirmed.                                          |\n(ns dao.stream.udp\n  \"The UDP channel of dao.stream.remote.md (3.2), rebuilt on the raw\n   datagram layer (docs/design/dao.stream.datagram.md 7): the WebSocket\n   deposit model over a socket that retains nothing, with the raw layer\n   beneath it feeding and carrying it.\n\n   Public facade delegating host-free value-channel logic to\n   `dao.stream.udp.common`.\"\n  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.udp.common :as common]))\n\n\n(def transport-type common/transport-type)\n(def max-datagram common/max-datagram)\n(def default-max-message-bytes common/default-max-message-bytes)\n(def default-max-partial-messages common/default-max-partial-messages)\n(def default-step-budget common/default-step-budget)\n\n(def descriptor? common/descriptor?)\n(def address-key common/address-key)\n(def make-port common/make-port)\n(def send-value! common/send-value!)\n(def send-to! common/send-to!)\n(def receive! common/receive!)\n(def projection common/projection)\n(def step! common/step!)\n(def reading-cursor common/reading-cursor)\n\n(defn port-step!\n  ([port] (common/port-step! port))\n  ([port budget] (common/port-step! port budget)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The writer handle and attacher\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(deftype UdpHandle\n  [port host dest-port descriptor]\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\n\n  (descriptor\n    [_]\n    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n     :dao.stream/descriptor descriptor\n     :dao.stream/identity (:dao.stream/identity descriptor)})\n\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamWriter\n\n  (append!\n    [_ v]\n    (common/send-value! port host dest-port v)))\n\n\n(defn make-attacher\n  \"Construct the host-composed unary `attach!` entry point: `:port` is\n   an already-composed `make-port` value. `attach!` binds or reuses it\n   -- one port serves every descriptor a composition attaches through\n   it -- and answers at once with a writer handle whose `append!` sends\n   toward the descriptor's address.\"\n  [config]\n  (common/make-attacher ->UdpHandle config))\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp/common.cljc b/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp/common.cljc\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..c69d194c\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/src/cljc/dao/stream/udp/common.cljc\n@@ -0,0 +1,409 @@\n+(ns dao.stream.udp.common\n+  \"Shared host-free value-channel functions and state for dao.stream.udp:\n+   fragmentation, reassembly, make-port, send-value!, receive!, projection,\n+   step!, reading-cursor, and port-step!.\"\n+  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]\n+            [dao.stream.base64 :as base64]\n+            [dao.stream.chunks :as chunks]\n+            [dao.stream.cbor :as cbor]\n+            [dao.stream.datagram :as datagram])\n+  #?(:cljd (:import [\"dart:typed_data\" Uint8List])))\n+\n+\n+(def transport-type :dao.stream/udp)\n+\n+\n+(def max-datagram\n+  \"The whole encoded datagram's budget, fragment envelope included: the\n+   1200-byte figure dao.jing.dht also uses to clear common path\n+   MTUs (docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md 3.2).\"\n+  1200)\n+\n+\n+(def default-max-message-bytes\n+  \"64 KiB, the default reassembly maximum (3.2).\"\n+  (* 64 1024))\n+\n+\n+(def default-max-partial-messages\n+  \"No default is named in 3.2; this is this implementation's own bound\n+   on outstanding partial messages, chosen only to keep reassembly\n+   memory finite without a clock.\"\n+  64)\n+\n+\n+(defn descriptor?\n+  \"{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity id\n+     :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p} (3.2).\"\n+  [x]\n+  (and (map? x)\n+       (= transport-type (:dao.stream/type x))\n+       (string? (:dao.stream/identity x))\n+       (string? (:dao.stream.udp/host x))\n+       (integer? (:dao.stream.udp/port x))\n+       (pos? (:dao.stream.udp/port x))))\n+\n+\n+(defn address-key\n+  \"The attachment identity a socket serves for one remote address: the\n+   host:port it saw, as a string, so it can key a map.\"\n+  [host port]\n+  (str host \":\" port))\n+\n+\n+;; =============================================================================\n+;; Portable host byte arrays\n+;; =============================================================================\n+\n+(defn- blen\n+  [bs]\n+  #?(:cljd (.-length ^Uint8List bs)\n+     :default (alength bs)))\n+\n+\n+;; =============================================================================\n+;; Fragmentation (write side)\n+;; =============================================================================\n+\n+(defn- direction-of\n+  \"A request carries :dao.stream.remote/op; an answer never does.\"\n+  [v]\n+  (if (contains? v :dao.stream.remote/op)\n+    :dao.stream.udp/request\n+    :dao.stream.udp/answer))\n+\n+\n+(defn- fragment-envelope\n+  [id part parts direction chunk]\n+  {:dao.stream.remote/id id\n+   :dao.stream.udp/part part\n+   :dao.stream.udp/parts parts\n+   :dao.stream.udp/direction direction\n+   :dao.stream.udp/bytes chunk})\n+\n+\n+(defn- fragments\n+  [payload id direction]\n+  (chunks/split payload max-datagram\n+                (fn [part parts bytes]\n+                  (fragment-envelope id part parts direction bytes))\n+                cbor/encode))\n+\n+\n+;; =============================================================================\n+;; The port: shared local-socket protocol state\n+;; =============================================================================\n+\n+(defn make-port\n+  \"Compose one local socket's protocol state. `:send!` is the host seam,\n+   `(send! host port bytes) -> an outcome map or nothing`, called once per\n+   outbound datagram; it must not wait, and a transport failure it answers\n+   travels up as `send-value!`'s outcome -- the raw writer's first non-ok\n+   outcome, not discarded. The composition composes it, over the raw\n+   datagram layer, as the Base64 closure appending one outbound value to\n+   `dao.stream.datagram/writer` (dao.stream.datagram.md 7). `:traffic` is\n+   the shared deposit target the composition wires -- a writer every\n+   reassembled datagram's event is appended to (`dao.stream/writer?`).\n+   `:raw-traffic` is the raw datagram layer's traffic ring the port reads\n+   itself a cursor on, minted :oldest at composition time so a raw gap\n+   reports lost datagrams -- `port-step!` requires it. `:max-message-bytes`\n+   and `:max-partial-messages` are the two reassembly bounds (defaults\n+   above).\"\n+  [{:keys [send! traffic raw-traffic max-message-bytes\n+           max-partial-messages]}]\n+  (when-not (and (fn? send!) (stream/writer? traffic))\n+    (throw (ex-info \"invalid dao.stream.udp port composition\"\n+                    {:send! send! :traffic traffic})))\n+  (when (and (some? raw-traffic) (not (stream/reader? raw-traffic)))\n+    (throw (ex-info \"invalid dao.stream.udp raw traffic composition\"\n+                    {:raw-traffic raw-traffic})))\n+  (atom {:send! send!\n+         :traffic traffic\n+         :raw-traffic raw-traffic\n+         :raw-cursor (when raw-traffic\n+                       (:dao.stream/cursor\n+                         (stream/cursor raw-traffic stream/anchor-oldest)))\n+         :max-message-bytes (or max-message-bytes\n+                                default-max-message-bytes)\n+         :max-partial-messages (or max-partial-messages\n+                                   default-max-partial-messages)\n+         :partial {}\n+         :partial-order []}))\n+\n+\n+(defn- send-datagram!\n+  \"One datagram out through the composed :send! seam: the seam's own\n+   outcome map travels up -- the raw writer's first non-ok outcome, not\n+   discarded -- and a fire-and-forget seam answering nothing is ok.\"\n+  [port host dest-port bytes]\n+  (let [r ((:send! @port) host dest-port bytes)]\n+    (if (and (map? r) (contains? r :dao.stream/outcome))\n+      r\n+      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok})))\n+\n+\n+(defn send-value!\n+  \"Encode and send `v` toward `host`:`dest-port` from `port`'s socket:\n+   a single datagram when it fits the budget, fragments when it does\n+   not, transport-error naming oversize when the encoded message\n+   exceeds :dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes -- never sent, torn or\n+   partially sent. The seam's first non-ok outcome is the answer: a\n+   fragment run stops there, cleanly, nothing torn following it.\"\n+  [port host dest-port v]\n+  (let [payload (cbor/encode v)\n+        n (blen payload)]\n+    (cond\n+      (> n (:max-message-bytes @port))\n+      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n+       :dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/oversize}\n+\n+      (<= n max-datagram)\n+      (send-datagram! port host dest-port payload)\n+\n+      :else\n+      (let [id (:dao.stream.remote/id v)\n+            direction (direction-of v)]\n+        (loop [frags (fragments payload id direction)]\n+          (if (empty? frags)\n+            {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}\n+            (let [r (send-datagram! port host dest-port\n+                                    (cbor/encode (first frags)))]\n+              (if (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n+                (recur (rest frags))\n+                r))))))))\n+\n+\n+(defn send-to!\n+  \"The explicit-destination write path from an already-bound port,\n+   outside any attach!'d descriptor -- hole punching depends on it\n+   (3.2, section 4).\"\n+  [port host dest-port v]\n+  (send-value! port host dest-port v))\n+\n+\n+;; =============================================================================\n+;; Reassembly (read side)\n+;; =============================================================================\n+\n+(defn- fragment-envelope?\n+  \"Any reserved fragment key present makes a datagram a fragment\n+   attempt: it must then validate fully or be dropped -- a shape\n+   carrying :parts/:direction/:bytes but missing :part does not fall\n+   through to the deposit path.\"\n+  [v]\n+  (and (map? v)\n+       (some #(contains? v %)\n+             [:dao.stream.udp/part :dao.stream.udp/parts\n+              :dao.stream.udp/direction :dao.stream.udp/bytes])))\n+\n+\n+(defn- well-formed-fragment?\n+  \"A fragment envelope's own wire fields, checked before any reassembly\n+   state can change: :part and :parts are integers with 0 <= :part <\n+   :parts, 1 <= :parts, :direction one of the two the write side\n+   mints, and :bytes a host byte payload. A datagram failing this is\n+   best-effort wire input, dropped below the mirror and the link like\n+   any other malformed one -- nothing here may throw during\n+   reassembly, whatever a peer put on the wire.\"\n+  [v]\n+  (let [{:dao.stream.udp/keys [part parts direction bytes]} v]\n+    (and (integer? part)\n+         (integer? parts)\n+         (pos? parts)\n+         (<= 0 part)\n+         (< part parts)\n+         (#{:dao.stream.udp/request :dao.stream.udp/answer} direction)\n+         (cbor/byte-payload? bytes))))\n+\n+\n+(defn- absorb-fragment!\n+  [port attachment frag]\n+  (let [{:dao.stream.remote/keys [id]\n+         :dao.stream.udp/keys [part parts direction bytes]} frag\n+        k [attachment direction id]\n+        [next-state payload]\n+        (chunks/absorb @port k part parts bytes\n+                       (:max-message-bytes @port)\n+                       (:max-partial-messages @port))]\n+    (reset! port next-state)\n+    payload))\n+\n+\n+(defn- deposit!\n+  [port attachment host src-port v]\n+  (stream/append! (:traffic @port)\n+                  {:dao.stream.udp/attachment attachment\n+                   :dao.stream.udp/source {:dao.stream.udp/host host\n+                                           :dao.stream.udp/port src-port}\n+                   :dao.stream.udp/value v}))\n+\n+\n+(defn receive!\n+  \"Adapter entry: one raw datagram arrived from `host`:`src-port` on\n+   `port`'s socket, as `bytes`. Malformed CBOR is dropped as a\n+   diagnostic below the mirror and the link, per dao.stream.remote.md\n+   2.1, and so is a fragment envelope whose own fields are malformed\n+   -- checked before any partial state can change. A plain value\n+   deposits at once; a fragment envelope folds into reassembly and\n+   deposits only once complete -- loss of any part is loss of the\n+   whole message, recovered by the link's resend rule, not by this\n+   port. Replies to what this call learns go to `host`:`src-port`,\n+   this datagram's own source, never an address a payload might\n+   claim.\"\n+  [port host src-port bytes]\n+  (when-some [v (try (cbor/decode bytes) (catch #?(:cljd Object\n+                                                   :clj Throwable\n+                                                   :cljs :default) _\n+                                           nil))]\n+    (let [attachment (address-key host src-port)]\n+      (if (fragment-envelope? v)\n+        (when (well-formed-fragment? v)\n+          (when-some [payload (absorb-fragment! port attachment v)]\n+            (when-some [whole (try (cbor/decode payload)\n+                                   (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable\n+                                             :cljs :default) _\n+                                     nil))]\n+              (deposit! port attachment host src-port whole))))\n+        (deposit! port attachment host src-port v)))))\n+\n+\n+;; =============================================================================\n+;; The projection: one channel ring per attachment (this channel's\n+;; ws-project)\n+;; =============================================================================\n+\n+(defn projection\n+  \"Compose one projection over the attachment identity `:attachment`\n+   (an `address-key`), the shared traffic reader `:traffic` this port\n+   deposits onto, the already-minted reading cursor `:cursor`, and the\n+   channel ring `:ring` this attachment's reflections and mirror steps\n+   read. Owns the cursor from here: each `step!` advances it.\"\n+  [{:keys [attachment traffic cursor ring]}]\n+  (when-not (and (string? attachment)\n+                 (stream/reader? traffic)\n+                 (some? cursor)\n+                 (stream/reader? ring)\n+                 (stream/writer? ring))\n+    (throw (ex-info \"invalid dao.stream.udp projection composition\"\n+                    {:attachment attachment :traffic traffic\n+                     :cursor cursor :ring ring})))\n+  (atom {:attachment attachment :traffic traffic :cursor cursor\n+         :ring ring}))\n+\n+\n+(defn step!\n+  \"Drive the projection once, at the composition's cadence: read the\n+   traffic medium to blocked, keeping this attachment's own events and\n+   dropping every other attachment's, appending each kept value onto\n+   the channel ring. A gap adopts the medium's own recovery cursor.\n+   Returns the projection.\"\n+  [project]\n+  (loop []\n+    (let [s @project\n+          r (stream/next (:traffic s) (:cursor s))]\n+      (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n+        :dao.stream/ok\n+        (let [event (:dao.stream/value r)]\n+          (swap! project assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n+          (when (= (:attachment s) (:dao.stream.udp/attachment event))\n+            (stream/append! (:ring s) (:dao.stream.udp/value event)))\n+          (recur))\n+\n+        :dao.stream/gap\n+        (do (swap! project assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n+            (recur))\n+\n+        project))))\n+\n+\n+(defn reading-cursor\n+  [project]\n+  (:cursor @project))\n+\n+\n+;; =============================================================================\n+;; Reading the raw datagram layer: port-step! (dao.stream.datagram.md 7)\n+;; =============================================================================\n+\n+(def default-step-budget\n+  \"The default `port-step!` budget: raw values consumed in one step when\n+   the composition names no bound.\"\n+  64)\n+\n+\n+(defn- dht-owned?\n+  \"True when `bytes` decode to a map carrying :dao.jing.dht/v -- the DHT\n+   interpreter's own datagram on a shared socket\n+   (dao.stream.datagram.md 6), dropped by this channel before its\n+   deposit. This one decode is the price of `receive!`'s fixed\n+   bytes-taking shape; receive! decodes again.\"\n+  [bytes]\n+  (when-some [v (try (cbor/decode bytes)\n+                     (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)\n+                            _\n+                       nil))]\n+    (and (map? v) (contains? v :dao.jing.dht/v))))\n+\n+\n+(defn port-step!\n+  \"One step reading the raw datagram layer's traffic ring into this port:\n+   read to `blocked` from the port's own raw cursor, bounded by `budget`\n+   values, skip lifecycle events and `:dao.jing.dht/v` maps, decode each\n+   datagram event's Base64 and call `receive!` with the source address\n+   the socket observed and the bytes. A raw `gap` adopts the recovery\n+   cursor: those datagrams are lost, indistinguishable from network\n+   loss, and the link's resend rule recovers them exactly as it does for\n+   it. A value that is neither a datagram event nor a lifecycle event is\n+   not the raw layer's and is skipped. One owner per port drives this,\n+   and the composition drives it before the projections' `step!`.\"\n+  ([port] (port-step! port default-step-budget))\n+  ([port budget]\n+   (let [{:keys [raw-traffic]} @port]\n+     (when-not raw-traffic\n+       (throw (ex-info \"dao.stream.udp port-step! needs :raw-traffic\"\n+                       {})))\n+     (loop [left budget]\n+       (if (pos? left)\n+         (let [r (stream/next raw-traffic (:raw-cursor @port))]\n+           (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n+             :dao.stream/ok\n+             (do (swap! port assoc :raw-cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n+                 (let [v (:dao.stream/value r)]\n+                   (when (datagram/datagram-event? v)\n+                     (let [bytes (base64/decode\n+                                   (:dao.stream.datagram/bytes v))]\n+                       (when-not (dht-owned? bytes)\n+                         (let [source (:dao.stream.datagram/source v)]\n+                           (receive! port\n+                                     (:dao.stream.datagram/host source)\n+                                     (:dao.stream.datagram/port source)\n+                                     bytes))))))\n+                 (recur (dec left)))\n+\n+             :dao.stream/gap\n+             (do (swap! port assoc :raw-cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n+                 (recur (dec left)))\n+\n+             port))\n+         port)))))\n+\n+\n+;; =============================================================================\n+;; Shared attacher helper\n+;; =============================================================================\n+\n+(defn make-attacher\n+  \"Construct the host-composed unary `attach!` entry point using the given\n+   handle-constructor `handle-ctor`.\"\n+  [handle-ctor {:keys [port] :as config}]\n+  (when-not (some? port)\n+    (throw (ex-info \"dao.stream.udp attacher needs :port\" {:config config})))\n+  (fn attach!\n+    [descriptor]\n+    (if-not (descriptor? descriptor)\n+      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor}\n+      (let [host (:dao.stream.udp/host descriptor)\n+            dest-port (:dao.stream.udp/port descriptor)]\n+        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n+         :dao.stream/handle (handle-ctor port host dest-port descriptor)\n+         :dao.stream/attachment (address-key host dest-port)}))))\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
